A||||2||RICK REIGSTAD||Installing high volume oil pump on 88GT
Z||000000||RICK REIGSTAD||05-31-99||10:43 AM||||I talked about having low oil pressure at idle and 30-40 psi at 2000 RPM. This bothered me to the point of installing a high volume oil pump made by sealed power. The first step was to raise the car enough on all four wheels so I could roll around under the car without my shoulders catching something.Next I removed the exhaust system.<br>Removed the starter,took the front motor mount bolts off,I jacked up the motor untill I could put a 2x4 between frame cradle and engine balancer. from here I could see that the front motor mount had to come off. This was somewhat of a pain because you will have to remove the alternater bracket.Now I could get at all the oil pan bolts and nuts. The pan came right off. I took off the oil pump-one bolt-and compared it with the new high volume unit I had purchased. The gears are taller in the high volume pump but the neck is shorter, so the final height is about the same.I installed a new screen pickup on the new pump-just because it is a good idea to have no trapped particals inside the screen and a new pipe will fit tighter to the pump. Before I removed the old pump I checked my screen to engine block height. It was 63/4 inches. Now I laid the new pan one piece gasket on the pan and measured the distance from bottom of pan to the top of new gasket. This was 7 inches, after I torque the oil pan down I would have a 3/16 oil screen to oil pan distance-well within spec. But before I primed my oil pump and installed it I thought this would be a great time to check my bottom end, after all this motor has 119,000 on the bottom end. I pulled the bearings off the crankshaft,all were standard size and looked like they were wearing evenly, so I plastic gaged them-they are in spec. but at the outer limits,so I replaced them with new . The rods were checked,all bearings where standard and wearing well, so I replaced them also. I used LUBRIPLATE NO. 105 motor assembly grease on all the new bearings-this is very important for minimizing the effects of dry starts. While I was down there I put my finger on the timing chain-what I could reach anyway- I could tell that there was alot more then 1/4 inch of slack in the chain. So a new timing chain and chain damper was odered.Removing the waterpump and then the timing cover proved to be a real pain in the butt! However with a little patience this can be done. The timing chain was trash and so was the damper.With the new timing chain installed I closed everything up.Installed oil and filter-crossed my fingers-turned the key and away she went.80 psi of oil pressure,got her to operating tempreture and put on 40-50 miles. Changed the oil and filter to make sure all LUBRIPLATE NO&gt;105 was gone.Althought I think it would have dissoved in the oil anyway. Now when I,am at idle the oil pressure is 40-50 at normal operating tempreture. This was alot of work but I have a sence of peace of mind.
Z||000001||theogre||06-01-99||07:59 AM||||Plastigauge is good for checking bearing clearance, but it's only a small part of the picture. there are other things like run out that it can't check.<p>if you were going that far, you should have at least had the crank polished. that would have made sure the thing was true and there are no hidden things to eat the new bearings. odds are good there are one or more journals out of round. the pre polish measuring would have caught that, and any other dimensional problems. 
Z||000002||GREENDANO||06-01-99||08:22 PM||||My new motor that I just rebuilt with a boat-load of parts has the exact same presures.  I'ld say your 119,000 miler is VERY GOOD!!
